From c05b68c34a7419a1b706b3e24f16a8abb6e4f13e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:54:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf: a record of numbers goes out in one struct and comes
 back into its members

The four `roottest/root/io/perf` buffers ran at 211-261 s of the 300 s limit
after round 6 closed them functionally, so any loaded machine turned them into
TIMEOUTs. `--jobs 1` and cProfile on the four said the cost was per record, not
per byte:

WriteBuffer. `TClass::Streamer` wrote each of nine members through
`_ELEMENTS` dispatch -> `_basic` -> `_number` -> `pack_one` -> `WBuffer.raw`,
nine `struct` calls and nine appends per record. `wpacking.pack_row` builds one
`struct` of every member's code when a record is all plain numbers, and
`_Writer.record` writes the whole row in one `pack`; a number `struct` refuses -
one too wide for its member, a float where an integer is declared - falls back to
the member-by-member writer, which wraps or truncates it as ROOT on file holds
it. The row plan is an `lru_cache` on (catalogue, class) rather than a
`WeakKeyDictionary`, whose lookup makes a weakref out of the catalogue per
record.

Both. `find_class` walked every class the interpreter had declared on every
call; the name a streamer prints is a key of `DECLARED` almost always, so it is
answered by a dictionary lookup and kept, validated by `DECLARED` still holding
that same class under that key - so a class declared again under one name is
answered afresh, which `test_a_class_declared_again_under_one_name_...` pins.

ReadBuffer. `_fill` ran nine times through `getattr(obj, attribute)` and a maker
closure each, whose `_scalar_number` re-masked an `int` that came off the buffer
already in range. `_fill` now hands the attribute a whole number of the member's
own C++ width directly (`declared._unchanged` names the range), and everything
else - a float where an integer is declared, a number too wide - goes the way it
always did.

Measured as CPU time of one in-process loop against the same binary at HEAD
(machine at load ~150, so wall clock says nothing): WriteBuffer 2.79 -> 1.58 s
per 2*10^5 records (1.77x), ReadBuffer 3.80 -> 1.72 s (2.21x). The bytes are
unchanged: the new tests pin a record of numbers against the member-by-member
writer, against ROOT's own wrapped and truncated values, and against the record
read back into an object.
